definition:
    French physicist (1900–1958) who ran the laboratory alongside his wife — and fellow Nobel laureate — Irène Joliot-Curie, daughter of Marie and Pierre Curie. In 1934 the couple bombarded aluminum with alpha particles and produced phosphorus-30: the first artificially created radioactive material. It opened the door to making radioisotopes on demand for medicine and research, and earned both of them the 1935 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. Frédéric later founded France's Atomic Energy Commission (CEA) and filed some of the earliest nuclear-reactor patent concepts.
